**Read:** [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1), chapters 8, 12 and 13 in full; [Advanced Thermodynamics — MIT 2.43, Spring 2024](../sources/Advanced-Thermodynamics-MIT-2-43.lingua#r_5GXQQ35FEFJM34SWA12X8QHGKC), multiphase equilibrium; [IAPWS-95 — fundamental water properties](../sources/IAPWS-95-Fundamental-Water-Formulation.lingua#r_4JH7YVKGMCC4R7MK1F5S1XY90Z), vapour-liquid equilibrium and phase limits.

At equilibrium, coexisting phases satisfy the appropriate equality of chemical potentials, with mechanical/thermal conditions and any interfacial effects stated. Derive the Clapeyron relation dp/dT = Δs/Δv = L/(TΔv) for a pure-substance coexistence curve, using consistent molar or mass-specific quantities.

The common Clausius–Clapeyron approximation adds assumptions about vapour behavior, liquid volume and latent-heat variation. State those before fitting a straight line to log pressure versus inverse temperature. A slope inferred from a narrow range need not remain valid across the entire phase diagram.

Count degrees of freedom with the applicable phase rule, species and constraints. Distinguish an equilibrium phase diagram from cooling-path kinetics, nucleation, glass formation and segregation. Use the same temperature/composition data to compare a thermodynamic phase claim with an actual process history.

